Abstract

Contemporary multimodal large language models (MLLMs)—exemplified by architectures such as GPT-4V and Gemini—demonstrate a critical failure mode when tasked with reasoning about temporally evolving physical systems. They frequently produce coherent but physically impossible reasoning chains, treat continuous-time dynamics as sequences of independent frames, and violate fundamental conservation laws like energy and momentum. Empirical benchmarks now consistently document this gap. This doctoral project proposes CogDynamics++, a mathematically rigorous architectural framework that integrates transfer operator theory (Koopman and Perron-Frobenius operators), physics-constrained neural architectures (Constraint-Projected Learning, Riemann Tensor Neural Networks), and Wasserstein-based uncertainty quantification to fundamentally address these failures. Our central thesis is that physical reasoning driven by visual data can be uniquely reformulated as a conservation-law-constrained dynamical system on a learned cognitive manifold. This thesis presents six novel theoretical contributions, including the formal Koopman-Transformer structural analogy theorem, a comprehensive theory of constraint-projected reasoning, and a Wasserstein-based bound on reasoning error propagation. Each theorem is grounded in peer-reviewed mathematical analysis from dynamical systems, deep learning theory, and uncertainty quantification. This document serves as the definitive theoretical blueprint; no empirical results are reported at this stage.

